//Bruteforce
- Time Complexity: \(O(n\log n)\)
- Space Complexity: \(O(\log n)\) auxiliary space for typical std::sort implementations.

class Solution {
public:
    int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int n = nums.size();
        return nums[n - k];
    }
};

//better

Time Complexity (TC): O(n + k log n)
- We iterate through all n elements and insert them into the max heap.
- Each insertion takes O(log n).
- Building the heap through n individual insertions takes O(n log n).
- We perform k-1 pop operations, each taking O(log n).
- Therefore, overall time complexity is O((n + k) log n), which simplifies to O(n log n).
Space Complexity (SC): O(n)
- The max heap stores all n elements.
- Therefore, auxiliary space complexity is O(n).

class Solution {
public:
    int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        priority_queue<int>pq;
        for(auto it : nums)
        {
            pq.push(it);
        }
        while(k != 1)
        {
            pq.pop();
            k--;
        }
        return pq.top();
    }
};




//optimal
**Time Complexity (TC): O(n log k)**

- We iterate through all n elements.
- Each pq.push() takes O(log k).
- Each pq.pop() takes O(log k).
- Therefore, overall time complexity is O(n log k).

**Space Complexity (SC): O(k)**

- The priority queue stores at most k elements.
- Therefore, auxiliary space complexity is O(k).


class Solution {
public:
    int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> pq;

        for (int num : nums) {
            pq.push(num);

            if (pq.size() > k) {
                pq.pop();
            }
        }

        return pq.top();
    }
};

